 Basic Questions on Properties Files
	1. What is a .properties file in Java?
		○ It is a plain text file used to store key-value pairs.
		○ Commonly used for storing configurations, such as URLs, credentials, timeouts, or browser settings.
	2. Why are properties files used in Selenium frameworks?
		○ To externalize test configuration so that changing values like URLs, browser types, or credentials doesn't require modifying the source code.
	3. What is the syntax of a properties file?
browser=chrome
baseUrl=https://example.com
username=admin
password=secret123

Code & Usage Questions
	1. How do you read values from a .properties file in Java?
Properties prop = new Properties();
FileInputStream fis = new FileInputStream("config.properties");
prop.load(fis);
String browser = prop.getProperty("browser");
	2. How do you use the properties in a Selenium test?
WebDriver driver;
if (browser.equalsIgnoreCase("chrome")) {
    driver = new ChromeDriver();
}
driver.get(prop.getProperty("baseUrl"));
	3. How do you handle missing or null property values?
		○ Use a default fallback:
String browser = prop.getProperty("browser", "chrome");

 Intermediate/Scenario-Based Questions
	1. Can you write/update a value in a .properties file?
prop.setProperty("status", "passed");
FileOutputStream out = new FileOutputStream("config.properties");
prop.store(out, "Test status updated");
	Great question!
	prop.store(out, "Test status updated");
	This line is used to save or write the key-value pairs from a Properties object to a .properties file (via the FileOutputStream out), with an optional comment at the top of the file.
	
	 Breakdown:
		• prop: An instance of the Properties class in Java.
		• store(...): A method in the Properties class that writes the properties to an output stream (e.g., file).
		• out: A FileOutputStream connected to the .properties file you're writing to.
		• "Test status updated": This is a comment that will appear at the top of the file (usually as a timestamped note).
	 Example:
	Suppose you run this code:
	Properties prop = new Properties();
prop.setProperty("status", "passed");
FileOutputStream out = new FileOutputStream("result.properties");
prop.store(out, "Test status updated");
out.close();
	The resulting result.properties file will look like this:
	#Test status updated
#Mon Apr 29 14:37:18 IST 2025
status=passed
	Use Case in Selenium:
	You might use this when you want to log test outcomes or environment states dynamically to a .properties file, e.g., in reporting or CI pipelines.
	
	2. How do you manage different environments using properties files?
		○ Use different files like:
			§ config-dev.properties
			§ config-test.properties
			§ config-prod.properties
		○ Load based on a parameter:
String env = System.getProperty("env", "dev");
FileInputStream fis = new FileInputStream("config-" + env + ".properties");
	3. How do you pass property values at runtime via command line or Maven?
mvn test -Denv=qa
Then read it in code:
String env = System.getProperty("env");

 Advanced/Design-Level Questions
	1. How do you design a framework to support dynamic configuration using properties?
	• Create a ConfigReader or PropertyManager class to:
		○ Load the appropriate file
		○ Provide typed getter methods
		○ Handle missing/invalid keys
	2. How do you cache or reuse loaded property files efficiently?
	• Load once in a static block or singleton:
private static Properties prop = new Properties();
static {
    try {
        FileInputStream fis = new FileInputStream("config.properties");
        prop.load(fis);
    } catch (IOException e) {
        e.printStackTrace();
    }
}

public static String get(String key) {
    return prop.getProperty(key);
}
	3. What are the limitations of using .properties files?
	• No structured hierarchy (unlike JSON or XML)
	• Not suitable for complex data types
	• No built-in support for encryption (e.g., storing secure credentials)
